/* The PEM reader named what stops it (PEM private keys plan, Task 3).
   %1$@ is one of the core.connect.pemFeature.* sentences below; %2$@ is the
   command line SSHKeyConverter builds — never a catalog string.
   `ssh-keygen -p` rewrites the file IN PLACE (SSHKeyConverter.inPlaceCommandLine
   says so); the button converts a COPY. The two clauses say which is which.
   The .noPath variant is for the dial that names no file at all: the form's
   key path row is empty because the PEM key was the JUMP hop's and the target
   authenticates some other way, so there is no path to quote and no command
   to offer. The bare tool name is not a command line — it takes no path — so
   the rule that command lines come from SSHKeyConverter is not in play. */
"core.connect.keyPEMNotReadable %@ %@" = "MacSCP cannot read this PEM key: %1$@. Convert it in place in the terminal with %2$@ (the passphrase stays), press Convert key… to let MacSCP convert a copy, or load the key into the ssh-agent and choose the agent as the login.";
